(*=============================================================================
  BrightScript Grammar (EBNF)
=============================================================================

  WHAT THIS IS
  ------------
  A comprehensive grammar for Roku's BrightScript language, intended as the
  authoritative specification from which a tree-sitter / ast-grep parser will
  be built. It covers the full *core* BrightScript language: lexical tokens,
  declarations, statements, the precedence-layered expression grammar, the
  type system, conditional compilation, and exception handling.

  This file describes plain BrightScript only. SceneGraph XML, BrighterScript
  extensions (classes, namespaces, import, ternary `?:`, `??`), and the
  component/global-function libraries are intentionally OUT OF SCOPE here.
  BrighterScript-only constructs are NOT modeled. A couple of constructs that
  exist in BrightScript but are version-gated (optional chaining, ++/--,
  compound assignment, LongInteger) ARE modeled and annotated with the Roku OS
  version that introduced them.

  SOURCE
  ------
  Roku BrightScript Language Reference (verified against the live docs):
    Hub ......................... https://developer.roku.com/dev/docs/brightscript-language-reference
    Expressions, variables, types https://developer.roku.com/dev/docs/expressions-variables-types
    Program statements .......... https://developer.roku.com/dev/docs/program-statements
    Statement summary ........... https://developer.roku.com/dev/docs/statement-summary
    Reserved words .............. https://developer.roku.com/dev/docs/reserved-words
    Conditional compilation ..... https://developer.roku.com/dev/docs/conditional-compilation
    Error handling .............. https://developer.roku.com/dev/docs/error-handling
    roXMLElement (.@ operator) .. https://developer.roku.com/docs/references/brightscript/components/roxmlelement.md

  Date: 2026-05-19

  EBNF NOTATION (W3C-style, as used in the XML spec)
  --------------------------------------------------
  Rules have the form:               Name ::= expression
  Reference to another rule:         Name
  Alternation:                       A | B
  Grouping:                          ( A B )
  Optional (zero or one):            A?
  Zero or more:                      A*
  One or more:                       A+
  Terminal string (literal token):   'literal'  or  "literal"
  Character class / range:           [a-zA-Z]  [0-9]  [^"]
  Exception ("A but not B"):         A - B
  Hex character code:                #x000A   (Unicode code point)
  Comments:                          (* ... *)  <-- THIS comment style only

  Convention used here:
    * UpperCamelCase ........ syntactic (parser) rules / non-terminals.
    * UPPER_SNAKE ........... lexical (token) rules / terminals produced by
                              the lexer (keywords, literals, punctuation).
    * 'lowercase' literals .. spelled keywords. BrightScript keywords are
                              CASE-INSENSITIVE; see the implementer notes at the
                              end. Literals are written lowercase here for
                              readability but match any case.

  HOW TO READ THE NEWLINE / TERMINATOR MODEL
  ------------------------------------------
  BrightScript is line-oriented. Statements are terminated by a NEWLINE or by a
  colon ':' (which separates multiple statements on one physical line). There is
  NO line-continuation character. The token NL below stands for "one statement
  terminator" and EOS ("end of statement") abstracts "NL or ':' or end-of-file",
  with runs of blank terminators collapsed. See implementer notes for how a
  tree-sitter scanner should treat significant newlines.

=============================================================================*)


(*===========================================================================*)
(*  PART 1 - LEXICAL GRAMMAR (TOKENS)                                         *)
(*===========================================================================*)

(*--- 1.1 Whitespace, newlines, statement terminators ----------------------*)

(* Horizontal whitespace is insignificant EXCEPT that it separates tokens and
   is significant for the lexer's longest-match rule. It is skipped between
   tokens (it does NOT terminate a statement). *)
WS              ::= ( #x20 | #x09 )+                    (* space, tab *)

(* A physical line break. CRLF, CR, or LF all count as one. *)
LINE_BREAK      ::= #x0D #x0A | #x0A | #x0D

(* NL = a single statement terminator produced from a line break. *)
NL              ::= LINE_BREAK

(* EOS = "end of statement": a newline, a colon separator, or EOF. A run of
   consecutive terminators (blank lines, stray colons) collapses to one EOS.
   Used throughout the syntactic grammar to terminate statements.

   DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #14) on Roku OS 15.1.4: a newline is
   suppressed (NOT a terminator) only when nested inside the '[ ]' / '{ }'
   collection literals and inside call ARGUMENT LISTS. A newline inside a
   grouping '( )' DOES terminate -- 'x = (1 +<NL>2)' is a Syntax Error (&h02).
   So grouping parens do NOT extend a statement across lines; only []/{} /
   arg-lists do. See corpus/negative/lex_eos_depth0_paren.brs. *)
EOS             ::= ( NL | ':' )+

(*--- 1.2 Comments ---------------------------------------------------------*)

(* Two equivalent comment forms; both run to end of line. A comment is NOT a
   statement terminator by itself - the trailing NEWLINE terminates. *)
Comment         ::= ( "'" | REM_KW ) CommentText?
CommentText     ::= ( Char - LINE_BREAK )*
REM_KW          ::= 'rem'                               (* case-insensitive *)
(* NOTE: 'rem' as a comment introducer wins over identifier 'rem' via the
   keyword/longest-match rules; it is a reserved word. *)

(*--- 1.3 Identifiers ------------------------------------------------------*)

(* Start with a letter or underscore; continue with letters, digits, or
   underscore; MAY end with a single optional type-designator suffix.
   Case-insensitive. A bare identifier must not collide with a reserved word
   (see 1.9) - those are lexed as keywords instead. *)
Identifier      ::= IdentStart IdentCont* TypeSuffix?
IdentStart      ::= [a-zA-Z_]
IdentCont       ::= [a-zA-Z0-9_]
TypeSuffix      ::= '$' | '%' | '!' | '#' | '&'
                    (* $=String %=Integer !=Float #=Double &=LongInteger.
                       '&' suffix requires Roku OS 7.0+. The suffix is part of
                       the identifier token (e.g. count% and count are the same
                       variable conceptually, but the suffix declares type). *)

(*--- 1.4 Numeric literals -------------------------------------------------*)

(* BrightScript infers numeric literal type by form:
     - 10+ digits, or 'D' exponent, or '#' suffix  => Double
     - contains '.' or 'E' exponent or '!' suffix   => Float
     - hex &h... or plain digits                    => Integer
     - trailing '&' suffix, or 11+ digit hex        => LongInteger
   We model the distinct lexical shapes; the type-inference is a semantic note. *)

(* A decimal integer literal may carry the explicit '%' integer type designator
   (e.g. 1%), mirroring '!'(Float)/'#'(Double)/'&'(LongInteger) on the other forms
   (bsc-confirmed). Hex form takes no '%'. *)
IntegerLiteral      ::= DecDigits '%'?
                      | HexLiteral
DecDigits           ::= [0-9]+

(* Hexadecimal integer literal: &h or &H prefix. *)
HexLiteral          ::= ( '&h' | '&H' ) HexDigits
HexDigits           ::= [0-9a-fA-F]+

(* LongInteger literal (Roku OS 7.0+): decimal or hex with trailing '&'. *)
LongIntegerLiteral  ::= ( DecDigits | HexLiteral ) '&'

(* Float literal: decimal point and/or 'E' exponent, optional '!' suffix.
   Also a plain integer-looking number followed by '!' is a Float. *)
FloatLiteral        ::= ( DecDigits '.' DecDigits? FloatExp?
                        | '.' DecDigits FloatExp?
                        | DecDigits FloatExp
                        | DecDigits '!' )
                        '!'?
FloatExp            ::= ( 'e' | 'E' ) Sign? DecDigits

(* Double literal: 'D' exponent, or '#' suffix, or 10+ digit constant. *)
DoubleLiteral       ::= ( DecDigits '.' DecDigits? DoubleExp?
                        | '.' DecDigits DoubleExp?
                        | DecDigits DoubleExp
                        | DecDigits '.' DecDigits? '#'
                        | DecDigits '#' )
DoubleExp           ::= ( 'd' | 'D' ) Sign? DecDigits

Sign                ::= '+' | '-'

(* Aggregate token used by the expression grammar. *)
NumericLiteral      ::= DoubleLiteral
                      | FloatLiteral
                      | LongIntegerLiteral
                      | HexLiteral
                      | IntegerLiteral
                    (* ORDER MATTERS for longest-match: try Double/Float/Long
                       shapes before plain Integer; try Hex before bareword.
                       A tree-sitter scanner should resolve via maximal munch. *)

(*--- 1.5 String literals --------------------------------------------------*)

(* Double-quoted. An embedded double quote is written as two double quotes
   (""). There are NO backslash escape sequences in BrightScript strings;
   "" is the only escape. Strings cannot span lines. *)
StringLiteral   ::= '"' ( StringChar | EscapedQuote )* '"'
StringChar      ::= Char - ( '"' | LINE_BREAK )
EscapedQuote    ::= '""'

(*--- 1.6 Boolean and invalid literals -------------------------------------*)

BooleanLiteral  ::= 'true' | 'false'                   (* case-insensitive *)
InvalidLiteral  ::= 'invalid'                           (* case-insensitive *)

(*--- 1.7 Generic character ------------------------------------------------*)

Char            ::= [#x09#x0A#x0D#x20-#x10FFFF]         (* any Unicode char *)

(*--- 1.8 Operators and punctuation (terminals) ----------------------------*)

(* Listed here so the lexer and the tree-sitter author have an explicit token
   inventory. Multi-char operators must be matched before their prefixes. *)

(* Arithmetic *)
OP_PLUS         ::= '+'
OP_MINUS        ::= '-'
OP_STAR         ::= '*'
OP_SLASH        ::= '/'                                 (* float division *)
OP_BACKSLASH    ::= '\'                                 (* integer division *)
OP_CARET        ::= '^'                                 (* exponentiation *)
OP_MOD          ::= 'mod'                               (* keyword operator *)

(* Bit shift (Roku OS supports integer bitshift) *)
OP_SHL          ::= '<<'
OP_SHR          ::= '>>'

(* Comparison *)
OP_EQ           ::= '='
OP_NE           ::= '<>'
OP_LT           ::= '<'
OP_GT           ::= '>'
OP_LE           ::= '<='
OP_GE           ::= '>='

(* Logical / bitwise (keyword operators) *)
OP_AND          ::= 'and'
OP_OR           ::= 'or'
OP_NOT          ::= 'not'

(* Assignment and compound assignment (compound forms: Roku OS 7.1+) *)
ASSIGN          ::= '='
PLUS_ASSIGN     ::= '+='
MINUS_ASSIGN    ::= '-='
STAR_ASSIGN     ::= '*='
SLASH_ASSIGN    ::= '/='
BACKSLASH_ASSIGN::= '\='
SHL_ASSIGN      ::= '<<='
SHR_ASSIGN      ::= '>>='

(* Increment / decrement (Roku OS 7.1+) *)
OP_INCR         ::= '++'
OP_DECR         ::= '--'

(* Member / index / call / attribute / optional-chaining *)
DOT             ::= '.'
AT              ::= '@'                                 (* XML attribute access *)
LBRACKET        ::= '['
RBRACKET        ::= ']'
LPAREN          ::= '('
RPAREN          ::= ')'
LBRACE          ::= '{'
RBRACE          ::= '}'
COMMA           ::= ','
SEMI            ::= ';'
COLON           ::= ':'

(* Optional chaining operators (Roku OS 11.0+) *)
OC_DOT          ::= '?.'                                (* optional member *)
OC_AT           ::= '?@'                                (* optional XML attr *)
OC_BRACKET      ::= '?['                                (* optional index *)
OC_PAREN        ::= '?('                                (* optional call *)

(* Print alias *)
QUESTION        ::= '?'                                 (* shorthand for print *)

(* Preprocessor / conditional-compilation markers (the leading '#' begins a
   directive token; see 4.x). *)
HASH            ::= '#'

(*--- 1.9 Reserved words ---------------------------------------------------*)

(* The documented reserved-word set (case-insensitive). These cannot be used
   as plain variable names. Note 'mod', 'and', 'or', 'not' double as operators.
   Source: Reserved words page. *)
ReservedWord    ::= 'and' | 'box' | 'createobject' | 'dim' | 'each' | 'else'
                  | 'elseif' | 'end' | 'endfunction' | 'endif' | 'endsub'
                  | 'endwhile' | 'eval' | 'exit' | 'exitwhile' | 'false'
                  | 'for' | 'function' | 'getglobalaa'
                  | 'getlastruncompileerror' | 'getlastrunruntimeerror'
                  | 'goto' | 'if' | 'invalid' | 'let' | 'line_num' | 'next'
                  | 'not' | 'objfun' | 'or' | 'pos' | 'print' | 'rem'
                  | 'return' | 'run' | 'step' | 'stop' | 'sub' | 'tab'
                  | 'then' | 'to' | 'true' | 'type' | 'while'

(* Soft / contextual keywords: these are documented as language constructs but
   are NOT in the formal reserved-word list (so they can sometimes be used as
   identifiers depending on context). Treat as contextual keywords. *)
ContextualKeyword ::= 'mod' | 'as' | 'in' | 'throw' | 'try' | 'catch'
                    | 'library' | 'continue' | 'integer' | 'longinteger'
                    | 'float' | 'double' | 'string' | 'boolean' | 'object'
                    | 'dynamic' | 'void' | 'interface'
(* NOTE: 'end function', 'end sub', 'end if', 'end while', 'end for',
   'end try' are written with an optional space; both 'endfunction' and
   'end function' are accepted. See implementer notes. *)


(*===========================================================================*)
(*  PART 2 - SYNTACTIC GRAMMAR: PROGRAM STRUCTURE & DECLARATIONS             *)
(*===========================================================================*)

(*--- 2.1 Program / source file --------------------------------------------*)

(* A source file is a sequence of top-level items: function/sub declarations,
   library statements, conditional-compilation directives, comments, blank
   lines, and (legacy) bare statements / labels at file scope. *)
SourceFile      ::= EOS? ( TopLevelItem ( EOS TopLevelItem )* )? EOS?

TopLevelItem    ::= FunctionDeclaration
                  | SubDeclaration
                  | LibraryStatement
                  | ConstDirective
                  | ConditionalCompilation
                  | Comment
                  | Label
                  | Statement
                (* Bare top-level Statements are accepted by the runtime
                   (legacy global scope); modern code keeps logic in functions. *)

(*--- 2.2 Library statement ------------------------------------------------*)

(* Imports a system or external .brs library by path. *)
LibraryStatement ::= 'library' StringLiteral

(*--- 2.3 Function & Sub declarations ---------------------------------------*)

(* A named function. Return type via 'as Type' is optional (defaults Dynamic).
   'function' ... 'end function' (or 'endfunction').
   DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #5) on Roku OS 15.1.4: a named function/sub
   declaration is a TOP-LEVEL item ONLY. A *nested NAMED* function (a
   FunctionDeclaration/SubDeclaration appearing inside another function body) is
   REJECTED at compile time (Syntax Error &h02). Anonymous function VALUES
   (AnonymousFunction, see 5.5) assigned/passed inside a function ARE valid -- so
   the rejection is specifically of the named-declaration form in statement
   position, not of function literals. See Statement (3.1) where nested decls are
   annotated out. *)
FunctionDeclaration ::=
        'function' Identifier LPAREN ParameterList? RPAREN ReturnType?
            EOS
            Block?
        EndFunction

(* A named sub: a function whose return type is implicitly Void.
   A return type may still be written but must be 'void' if present. *)
SubDeclaration ::=
        'sub' Identifier LPAREN ParameterList? RPAREN ReturnType?
            EOS
            Block?
        EndSub

EndFunction     ::= 'end' 'function' | 'endfunction'
EndSub          ::= 'end' 'sub' | 'endsub'

(* 'as Type' return-type annotation. *)
ReturnType      ::= 'as' Type

(*--- 2.4 Parameters --------------------------------------------------------*)

(* Parameters are comma-separated. Each may have a default value and/or an
   'as Type' annotation. Per docs: once a parameter has a default, all
   subsequent parameters must also have defaults (a semantic rule, not encoded
   here). Order within a single parameter: name, then optional '= default',
   then optional 'as Type'. *)
ParameterList   ::= Parameter ( COMMA Parameter )*
Parameter       ::= Identifier ( ASSIGN Expression )? ( 'as' Type )?

(*--- 2.5 Type system -------------------------------------------------------*)

(* Type names used in 'as Type' annotations and parameter types.
   All are case-insensitive. 'Object' covers component references such as
   roArray / roAssociativeArray. 'Dynamic' is the unconstrained default.

   PARSE vs LINT (implementer note): structurally, Type is ANY identifier -- the
   'as' clause accepts any name and PARSES it cleanly. The intrinsic type set
   below is therefore the set of VALID types, enforced by a LINT/SEMANTIC rule,
   NOT by the grammar. Restricting Type to the enum at parse time would turn
   device-invalid annotations into ERROR nodes; instead the parser accepts them so
   a linter can flag them. (The intrinsic names, being non-reserved words, already
   match the `Identifier` alternative; only the two reserved keywords 'function'
   and 'invalid' need their own alternatives so they parse as a Type at all.)

   DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #6, #7) on Roku OS 15.1.4: ONLY the intrinsic
   type set below is valid in an 'as' clause. 'as interface' is rejected at compile
   time (&ha7, #6), and 'as <ComponentType>' / any non-intrinsic custom or
   component type name (e.g. 'as roSGNode') is also rejected (&ha7, #7) -- custom
   types in 'as' are a BrighterScript transpile feature, not device BrightScript.
   These DEVICE-INVALID names still PARSE (via the `Identifier` alternative); the
   intrinsic-only restriction is a lint check, not a parse constraint. *)
Type            ::= 'integer'                            (* --- VALID intrinsics: the *)
                  | 'longinteger'                       (* Roku OS 7.0+ *)
                  | 'float'                              (* device-accepted type set, *)
                  | 'double'                             (* enforced by LINT not parse *)
                  | 'string'
                  | 'boolean'
                  | 'object'
                  | 'function'
                  | 'dynamic'
                  | 'void'
                  | 'invalid'
                  | Identifier  (* parse-level catch-all: any identifier (custom /
                                   component type name, e.g. roSGNode, or 'interface')
                                   PARSES here so it can be LINTED. Such non-intrinsic
                                   names are DEVICE-REJECTED &ha7 (DEVICE_FACTS.md #6,
                                   #7) -- see corpus/negative/decl_type_interface.brs
                                   and corpus/negative/decl_type_custom.brs. The
                                   intrinsic literals above are listed only to
                                   document the lint-valid set; being non-reserved,
                                   they also match this Identifier alternative. *)


(*===========================================================================*)
(*  PART 3 - SYNTACTIC GRAMMAR: STATEMENTS                                    *)
(*===========================================================================*)

(*--- 3.1 Statement blocks & the statement set ------------------------------*)

(* A Block is a sequence of statements separated by statement terminators.
   Labels and comments may appear interspersed.

   SINGLE-LINE ':'-JOINED DECLARATIONS: because EOS = ( NL | ':' )+ (see Part 1),
   the ':' is itself a statement terminator, so several BlockItems may share one
   physical line, e.g. a function/anonymous-function value declared then used on
   one line: `f = function() ... end function : g = function() ... end function`.
   This is ALREADY grammatical via `( BlockItem EOS )*` with EOS => ':'; no extra
   rule is needed. (NOTE: this is distinct from InlineStatements in 3.4, which is
   the ':'-join specifically AFTER `then` in a SingleLineIf.) Confirmed by the
   angel-roku audit. See coverage leaf decl.anon.singleline_colon.

   LABEL INSIDE A LOOP: a Label is a BlockItem, and a loop body is a Block, so a
   label may appear inside a for/while body, e.g.
       for i = 1 to 3
         retry:
         ...
       end for
   This is ALREADY grammatical (Label is in BlockItem). Confirmed by the
   angel-roku audit. See coverage leaf stmt.label.in_loop. *)
Block           ::= ( BlockItem EOS )* BlockItem?
BlockItem       ::= Statement | Label | Comment | ConditionalCompilation

Statement       ::= AssignmentStatement
                  | CompoundAssignStatement
                  | IncDecStatement
                  | PrintStatement
                  | IfStatement
                  | ForStatement
                  | ForEachStatement
                  | WhileStatement
                  | ExitStatement
                  | ContinueStatement
                  | ReturnStatement
                  | DimStatement
                  | GotoStatement
                  | StopStatement
                  | EndStatement
                  | TryStatement
                  | ThrowStatement
                  | ExpressionStatement
                (* DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #5) on Roku OS 15.1.4:
                   nested NAMED FunctionDeclaration / SubDeclaration are NOT valid
                   statements -- declaring a named function inside another function
                   is a Syntax Error (&h02). They are therefore removed from the
                   Statement set (named declarations are top-level only, see
                   TopLevelItem in 2.1). To use a function as a value inside a body,
                   use an AnonymousFunction expression (5.5), which IS valid. *)

(*--- 3.2 Assignment --------------------------------------------------------*)

(* Plain assignment. The LHS is an assignable l-value: a variable or a
   member/index target. DEVICE-CONFIRMED on Roku OS 15.1.4: a leading 'let' is
   NOT accepted -- 'let x = 5' is a Syntax Error (compile error &h02). 'let'
   stays a reserved word (it cannot be a variable name) but the LET-assignment
   form is unsupported. (BrighterScript agrees: BS1081.) *)
AssignmentStatement ::= AssignTarget ASSIGN Expression

AssignTarget    ::= Identifier PostfixSuffix*
                    (* e.g. x  |  m.count  |  arr[i]  |  obj.list[2].name *)

(* Compound assignment (Roku OS 7.1+). *)
CompoundAssignStatement ::= AssignTarget CompoundOp Expression
CompoundOp      ::= PLUS_ASSIGN | MINUS_ASSIGN | STAR_ASSIGN | SLASH_ASSIGN
                  | BACKSLASH_ASSIGN | SHL_ASSIGN | SHR_ASSIGN

(* Increment / decrement (Roku OS 7.1+). Postfix only. *)
IncDecStatement ::= AssignTarget ( OP_INCR | OP_DECR )

(*--- 3.3 Print -------------------------------------------------------------*)

(* 'print' or '?' shorthand. Print items are separated by ',' (advance to next
   tab zone), ';' (no separator), OR whitespace alone (an empty/whitespace
   separator that prints items adjacently). A trailing separator suppresses the
   newline. TAB(expr) and POS(expr) are special positional items. Empty print
   (just newline) is allowed. *)
PrintStatement  ::= ( 'print' | QUESTION ) PrintItemList?

(* The separator between two print items is OPTIONAL: a bare run of whitespace
   acts as a separator (the whitespace/empty alternative of PrintSep). So
   `? "x " a " y " b` prints four items separated only by spaces, and the
   `print tab(5) "x"` positional form is just two whitespace-separated items.
   Real Roku code relies on this (~30 sites in the angel-roku audit). *)
PrintItemList   ::= PrintItem ( PrintSep? PrintItem )* PrintSep?
PrintItem       ::= TabItem | PosItem | Expression
PrintSep        ::= SEMI | COMMA
                  (* The whitespace/empty separator alternative is modeled by the
                     PrintSep-optional in PrintItemList above: two adjacent
                     PrintItems with only whitespace between them are separated by
                     an "empty" PrintSep. For the tree-sitter implementer:
                     whitespace lives in `extras`, so an empty separator is just
                     two items in sequence with no SEMI/COMMA token between them. *)
TabItem         ::= 'tab' LPAREN Expression RPAREN
PosItem         ::= 'pos' LPAREN Expression RPAREN
(* NOTE: 'tab' and 'pos' are contextual here; outside print they are reserved
   words usable only as these positional functions. *)

(*--- 3.4 If ----------------------------------------------------------------*)

(* Two forms:
     (a) Block IF spanning multiple lines, terminated by 'end if'/'endif'.
     (b) Single-line IF: statement(s) follow THEN on the same line, optional
         single-line ELSE. 'then' is optional in both forms.
   The disambiguation between block and single-line forms is "is there a
   newline after the (optional THEN) clause?" - handled by the scanner. *)

IfStatement     ::= BlockIf | SingleLineIf

BlockIf         ::= 'if' Expression 'then'? EOS
                        Block?
                    ( ElseIfClause )*
                    ( ElseClause )?
                    EndIf
ElseIfClause    ::= ( 'else' 'if' | 'elseif' ) Expression 'then'? EOS
                        Block?
ElseClause      ::= 'else' EOS
                        Block?
EndIf           ::= 'end' 'if' | 'endif'

SingleLineIf    ::= 'if' Expression 'then'? InlineStatements
                    ( 'else' InlineStatements )?
(* InlineStatements = one or more statements joined by ':' on the same line,
   with no terminating newline before 'else'/end-of-line. *)
InlineStatements ::= Statement ( COLON Statement )*

(*--- 3.5 For (numeric) -----------------------------------------------------*)

(* counter = start TO end [STEP step]. Terminated by 'end for' or legacy
   'next' (optionally followed by the counter variable).
   DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #10) on Roku OS 15.1.4: both 'next' and
   'next <var>' (the counter variable after 'next') are VALID -- the channel
   compiled and ran with 'next i' present. (Resolves the earlier "next"
   unverified flag in grammar/CLAUDE.md.) NOTE a bsc-vs-device drift:
   BrighterScript (bsc) WRONGLY rejects 'next <var>' (BS1039/BS1066); the device
   accepts it. See grammar/BSC_DRIFT.md. *)
ForStatement    ::= 'for' Identifier ASSIGN Expression 'to' Expression
                        ( 'step' Expression )? EOS
                        Block?
                    ForTerminator
ForTerminator   ::= ( 'end' 'for' ) | 'endfor' | ( 'next' Identifier? )

(*--- 3.6 For Each ----------------------------------------------------------*)

(* Iterates an enumerable object (ifEnum). Terminated by 'end for'; 'next' is
   NOT permitted for for-each per docs (but legacy parsers tolerate it). *)
ForEachStatement ::= 'for' 'each' Identifier 'in' Expression EOS
                        Block?
                     ( ( 'end' 'for' ) | 'endfor' )

(*--- 3.7 While -------------------------------------------------------------*)

WhileStatement  ::= 'while' Expression EOS
                        Block?
                    EndWhile
EndWhile        ::= ( 'end' 'while' ) | 'endwhile'

(*--- 3.8 Exit / Continue ---------------------------------------------------*)

(* EXIT FOR / EXIT WHILE break out of the nearest enclosing loop. *)
ExitStatement   ::= ( 'exit' 'for' )
                  | ( 'exit' 'while' )
                  | 'exitwhile'                          (* legacy single token *)

(* CONTINUE FOR / CONTINUE WHILE skip to next iteration. *)
ContinueStatement ::= ( 'continue' 'for' ) | ( 'continue' 'while' )

(*--- 3.9 Return ------------------------------------------------------------*)

ReturnStatement ::= 'return' Expression?

(*--- 3.10 Dim --------------------------------------------------------------*)

(* DIM name[dim1, dim2, ...] - creates an roArray with the given max indices.
   Each dimension is an expression (max index).
   DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #8) on Roku OS 15.1.4: BRACKET bounds
   'dim a[n]' are REQUIRED; the paren form 'dim a(n)' is a Syntax Error (&h02).
   (Resolves the earlier "Dim with (...) vs [...]" unverified flag in
   grammar/CLAUDE.md.) The paren alternative is kept below only as an
   annotated/rejected form; see corpus/negative/stmt_dim_paren.brs. *)
DimStatement    ::= 'dim' Identifier DimBounds
DimBounds       ::= LBRACKET Expression ( COMMA Expression )* RBRACKET
              (* --- DEVICE-REJECTED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #8) -- paren bounds invalid:
                  | LPAREN   Expression ( COMMA Expression )* RPAREN --- *)

(*--- 3.11 Goto & Labels ----------------------------------------------------*)

GotoStatement   ::= 'goto' Identifier
(* A label is an identifier alone on a line followed by ':'. *)
Label           ::= Identifier COLON

(*--- 3.12 Stop / End -------------------------------------------------------*)

StopStatement   ::= 'stop'                               (* invoke debugger *)
EndStatement    ::= 'end'                                (* terminate program *)
(* CAUTION: bare 'end' must be disambiguated from 'end if'/'end for'/etc by the
   following token. A standalone 'end' (followed by EOS) terminates the program. *)

(*--- 3.13 Try / Catch ------------------------------------------------------*)

TryStatement    ::= 'try' EOS
                        Block?
                    'catch' Identifier EOS
                        Block?
                    EndTry
EndTry          ::= ( 'end' 'try' ) | 'endtry'

ThrowStatement  ::= 'throw' Expression
                    (* Expression is a String message or an AA with fields
                       like { number: ..., message: ... }. *)

(*--- 3.14 Expression statement --------------------------------------------*)

(* A standalone expression used for its side effects - in practice a function
   or method call. A bare identifier or member access with no call is NOT a
   valid statement: the expression's final postfix suffix MUST be a call. *)
ExpressionStatement ::= CallExpression

(* A Postfix expression whose LAST suffix is a plain call '(...)'. The "ends in a
   call" constraint is encoded structurally here so the rule is self-contained for
   the tree-sitter translation (it mirrors PostfixExpr in Part 5).
   DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #9) on Roku OS 15.1.4: an optional-call
   '?()' as a bare expression-STATEMENT (e.g. 'o.fn?()' on its own line) is a
   Syntax Error (&h02). It is therefore NOT a legal statement terminus and the
   OC_PAREN alternative is removed here. The '?()' optional-call IS valid in
   EXPRESSION position (e.g. 'y = f?()', see OptChainSuffix in Part 5 and the
   implementer notes). See corpus/negative/stmt_expr_optcall.brs. *)
CallExpression  ::= Primary PostfixSuffix* CallSuffix
              (* --- DEVICE-REJECTED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #9): an optional-call cannot
                  end a statement -- was: ... ( CallSuffix | OC_PAREN ArgumentList?
                  RPAREN ). The OC_PAREN form is statement-rejected; expression-
                  only (see OptChainSuffix). --- *)


(*===========================================================================*)
(*  PART 4 - CONDITIONAL COMPILATION (PREPROCESSOR DIRECTIVES)               *)
(*===========================================================================*)

(* Directives begin with '#' and occupy their own logical line. They are
   resolved at compile time. Constants are boolean (true/false) and may also be
   supplied by the manifest's bs_const. Note the spacing: '#else if' and
   '#end if' are written WITH a space (also accepted by some tools without). *)

ConditionalCompilation ::= IfDirectiveBlock | ErrorDirective

ConstDirective  ::= '#const' Identifier ASSIGN CCExpression

IfDirectiveBlock ::=
        '#if' CCExpression EOS
            BlockItemsCC?
        ( '#else' 'if' CCExpression EOS BlockItemsCC? )*
        ( '#else' EOS BlockItemsCC? )?
        EndIfDirective
EndIfDirective  ::= '#end' 'if' | '#endif'

ErrorDirective  ::= '#error' ErrorMessageText
ErrorMessageText ::= ( Char - LINE_BREAK )*

(* Within a conditional-compilation block, ordinary source lines (any
   BlockItem) appear and are conditionally included. *)
BlockItemsCC    ::= ( BlockItem EOS )* BlockItem?

(* The expression in a #if / #else if. DEVICE-CONFIRMED on Roku OS 15.1.4
   (Streaming Stick 4K): the ONLY legal forms are a single boolean literal or a
   single #const name -- the docs' "(True | False | <CONST-NAME>)". Boolean
   operators 'and'/'or'/'not', parentheses, and any compound expression are a
   COMPILE ERROR (&h93 "Invalid #If/#ElseIf expression"). Negation/conjunction
   must be expressed structurally via #else and nested #if. (BrighterScript
   agrees: BS1091.) *)
CCExpression    ::= BooleanLiteral | Identifier


(*===========================================================================*)
(*  PART 5 - EXPRESSION GRAMMAR (PRECEDENCE-LAYERED)                          *)
(*===========================================================================*)

(* Layered lowest-to-highest precedence. Each layer's comment states its
   associativity. This cascade mirrors the documented precedence table
   (highest -> lowest):
       1  ()                       grouping / call
       2  .                        member
       3  [ ]                      index
       4  ?. ?@ ?[ ?(             optional chaining (Roku OS 11+)
       5  ^                        exponentiation         (right-assoc)
       6  unary - +                negation               (right-assoc)
       7  * / mod \               multiplicative         (left-assoc)
       8  + -                      additive               (left-assoc)
       9  << >>                    bitshift               (left-assoc)
      10  < > = <> <= >=           comparison             (left-assoc, non-chaining)
      11  not                      logical/bitwise NOT    (right-assoc, unary)
      12  and                      logical/bitwise AND    (left-assoc, short-circuit)
      13  or                       logical/bitwise OR     (left-assoc, short-circuit)
   Layers 1-4 (call/member/index/optional-chaining) are grouped as the
   "Postfix" layer below since they are all left-associative postfix suffixes,
   tighter than every binary operator. Unary minus/plus binds looser than '^'
   per the table (so -2^2 = -(2^2)). *)

(* --- Top: Expression is the lowest-precedence layer --- *)
Expression      ::= OrExpr

(* Level 13: OR. Left-associative, short-circuit. *)
OrExpr          ::= AndExpr ( OP_OR AndExpr )*

(* Level 12: AND. Left-associative, short-circuit. *)
AndExpr         ::= NotExpr ( OP_AND NotExpr )*

(* Level 11: NOT (unary). Right-associative (prefix). *)
NotExpr         ::= OP_NOT NotExpr
                  | ComparisonExpr

(* Level 10: comparison. Left-associative; non-chaining in practice but the
   grammar permits chains (the runtime evaluates left to right). *)
ComparisonExpr  ::= BitshiftExpr ( ComparisonOp BitshiftExpr )*
ComparisonOp    ::= OP_LE | OP_GE | OP_NE | OP_EQ | OP_LT | OP_GT
                    (* Multi-char ops (<= >= <>) MUST be matched before the
                       single-char ops (< > =). *)

(* Level 9: bitshift. Left-associative. *)
BitshiftExpr    ::= AdditiveExpr ( ( OP_SHL | OP_SHR ) AdditiveExpr )*

(* Level 8: additive. Left-associative. '+' also concatenates strings. *)
AdditiveExpr    ::= MultiplicativeExpr ( ( OP_PLUS | OP_MINUS ) MultiplicativeExpr )*

(* Level 7: multiplicative. Left-associative. *)
MultiplicativeExpr ::= UnaryExpr ( MultiplicativeOp UnaryExpr )*
MultiplicativeOp ::= OP_STAR | OP_SLASH | OP_BACKSLASH | OP_MOD

(* Level 6: unary +/-. Right-associative (prefix). Binds LOOSER than '^'. *)
UnaryExpr       ::= ( OP_PLUS | OP_MINUS ) UnaryExpr
                  | PowerExpr

(* Level 5: exponentiation. RIGHT-associative. *)
PowerExpr       ::= PostfixExpr ( OP_CARET UnaryExpr )?
                    (* right-assoc: the RHS recurses through UnaryExpr so that
                       2 ^ -3 and 2 ^ 3 ^ 2 = 2 ^ (3 ^ 2) parse correctly. *)

(* Levels 1-4: postfix call / member / index / attribute / optional chaining.
   Left-associative; tightest binding. Applied as a chain of suffixes to a
   primary.

   IIFE (immediately-invoked function expression), e.g.
       (function() ... end function)()
   is ALREADY grammatical here with no special rule: an AnonymousFunction is a
   Primary, so a parenthesized one `(function() ... end function)` is a ParenExpr
   (also a Primary), and a trailing CallSuffix `()` is an ordinary PostfixSuffix
   applied to it. Confirmed: `Primary PostfixSuffix*` with Primary => ParenExpr
   and PostfixSuffix => CallSuffix covers the IIFE (17 sites in the angel-roku
   audit). See coverage leaf expr.iife.call. *)
PostfixExpr     ::= Primary PostfixSuffix*

PostfixSuffix   ::= CallSuffix
                  | IndexSuffix
                  | MemberSuffix
                  | AttributeSuffix
                  | OptChainSuffix

CallSuffix      ::= LPAREN ArgumentList? RPAREN
IndexSuffix     ::= LBRACKET Expression ( COMMA Expression )* RBRACKET
                    (* multi-subscript form arr[i, j] for dimmed arrays *)
MemberSuffix    ::= DOT Identifier
AttributeSuffix ::= AT Identifier            (* XML attribute access: node@attr *)

(* Optional-chaining suffixes (Roku OS 11.0+). Each short-circuits to invalid
   if the left side is invalid. DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #9) on Roku OS
   15.1.4: the OC_PAREN '?(args)' optional-call below is valid HERE, in EXPRESSION
   position (e.g. 'y = f?()'), but is REJECTED (&h02) as a bare expression
   statement -- see ExpressionStatement / CallExpression in 3.14. *)
OptChainSuffix  ::= OC_DOT Identifier                          (* ?.member *)
                  | OC_AT Identifier                           (* ?@attr *)
                  | OC_BRACKET Expression ( COMMA Expression )* RBRACKET (* ?[i] *)
                  | OC_PAREN ArgumentList? RPAREN              (* ?(args) -- expr only *)

ArgumentList    ::= Expression ( COMMA Expression )*

(*--- 5.1 Primary expressions ----------------------------------------------*)

Primary         ::= Literal
                  | Identifier
                  | 'm'                       (* the implicit instance AA 'm' *)
                  | ParenExpr
                  | ArrayLiteral
                  | AssocArrayLiteral
                  | AnonymousFunction
                  | CreateObjectCall
                  | BuiltinCall

ParenExpr       ::= LPAREN Expression RPAREN

(*--- 5.2 Literals ----------------------------------------------------------*)

Literal         ::= NumericLiteral
                  | StringLiteral
                  | BooleanLiteral
                  | InvalidLiteral
                  | LINE_NUM_Literal

LINE_NUM_Literal ::= 'line_num'   (* compiler-substituted current line number *)

(*--- 5.3 Array literal -----------------------------------------------------*)

(* [ ] roArray literal. Elements are expressions. Trailing comma tolerated by
   the runtime; modeled permissively. Elements may span lines inside [ ]. *)
ArrayLiteral    ::= LBRACKET EOS?
                        ( ArrayElement ( ElementSep ArrayElement )* ElementSep? )?
                    EOS? RBRACKET
ArrayElement    ::= Expression
ElementSep      ::= COMMA EOS? | EOS         (* comma and/or newline separate *)

(*--- 5.4 Associative array literal ----------------------------------------*)

(* { } roAssociativeArray literal. Keys are identifiers or string literals;
   value separated by ':'. Entries separated by ',' and/or newline -- the bare-
   EOS alternative of ElementSep (below) admits the COMMA-LESS, newline-separated
   form `{ a:1 <NL> b:2 }` (no commas, entries on their own lines), confirmed in
   real Roku code by the angel-roku audit. See coverage leaf expr.aa.newline_sep. *)
AssocArrayLiteral ::= LBRACE EOS?
                        ( AAEntry ( ElementSep AAEntry )* ElementSep? )?
                      EOS? RBRACE
AAEntry         ::= AAKey COLON Expression
AAKey           ::= Identifier | StringLiteral
                    (* A reserved word may be used as an AA key; it is treated
                       as a name in this position. *)

(*--- 5.5 Anonymous (function expression) -----------------------------------*)

(* function/sub literal assigned/passed as a value. No closures (per docs). *)
AnonymousFunction ::= AnonFunctionExpr | AnonSubExpr
AnonFunctionExpr ::= 'function' LPAREN ParameterList? RPAREN ReturnType? EOS
                        Block?
                     EndFunction
AnonSubExpr     ::= 'sub' LPAREN ParameterList? RPAREN ReturnType? EOS
                        Block?
                    EndSub

(*--- 5.6 CreateObject and selected built-in pseudo-functions ---------------*)

(* CreateObject is an ordinary global function call but is reserved, so it is
   modeled explicitly. Treated like a normal call for AST purposes. *)
CreateObjectCall ::= 'createobject' LPAREN ArgumentList? RPAREN

(* Other reserved built-ins that take call syntax. These are reserved words
   and so are recognized as calls (not user identifiers). Listed for the
   implementer; semantically they are ordinary global-function calls. *)
BuiltinCall     ::= ReservedBuiltinName LPAREN ArgumentList? RPAREN
ReservedBuiltinName ::= 'getglobalaa' | 'getlastruncompileerror'
                      | 'getlastrunruntimeerror' | 'eval' | 'objfun'
                      | 'run' | 'type' | 'box'


(*===========================================================================*)
(*  PART 6 - NOTES FOR THE TREE-SITTER IMPLEMENTER                            *)
(*===========================================================================*)
(*
  These are language quirks that EBNF cannot express cleanly. Address them in
  the tree-sitter `grammar.js` (and likely an external scanner in C).

  1. CASE-INSENSITIVITY.
     All keywords, type names, the reserved-word set, hex prefix &h/&H, the
     E/D exponent markers, and boolean/invalid literals are case-insensitive
     (FUNCTION == function == Function). EBNF literals above are written
     lowercase but match any case. In tree-sitter use case-insensitive token
     regexes (e.g. /[fF][uU][nN][cC][tT][iI][oO][nN]/ or token(prec(...,
     /function/i))). Identifiers themselves are case-insensitive for binding
     but preserve their written form in the AST.

  2. SIGNIFICANT NEWLINES / STATEMENT TERMINATION.
     BrightScript is line-oriented. A statement ends at a NEWLINE or a colon
     ':'. There is NO line-continuation character (no trailing '_' , no
     implicit continuation after a trailing operator or comma at top level).
     EXCEPTION: inside bracket/brace/paren groupings - array literals [ ],
     AA literals { }, argument lists ( ) - newlines are NOT terminators and
     may separate elements. An external scanner should track bracket depth and
     emit a "newline terminator" token only at depth 0. Model NL/EOS as an
     extern token from the scanner.

  3. LEXER LONGEST-MATCH (MAXIMAL MUNCH) ordering that the scanner must enforce:
       - '<=' '>=' '<>' before '<' '>' '='.
       - '<<=' '>>=' before '<<' '>>' before '<' '>'.
       - '+=' '-=' '*=' '/=' '\=' before '+' '-' '*' '/' '\'.
       - '++' '--' before '+' '-'.
       - '?.' '?@' '?[' '?(' (optional chaining) vs '?' (print alias): a '?'
         is optional-chaining ONLY when immediately (no space) followed by
         '.', '@', '[', or '('. Otherwise '?' begins a print statement.
         NOTE the documented BrighterScript ternary caveat: a space is REQUIRED
         after '?' before '[' or '(' to avoid being parsed as optional chaining.
       - Numeric literals: Double/Float/LongInteger shapes before plain Integer;
         the trailing type suffix ('!' '#' '&') and the 'D'/'E' exponent are
         part of the number token.
       - Type-designator suffix on identifiers ($ % ! # &) is part of the
         identifier token; ensure 'count%' lexes as one identifier, not
         'count' followed by '%' (BrightScript has no standalone '%' operator,
         which avoids ambiguity, but the scanner must still attach it).

  4. CONTEXTUAL / SOFT KEYWORDS.
     Several words are keywords only in context and are otherwise usable as
     identifiers: 'as', 'in', 'each', 'step', 'to', 'then', 'mod', 'tab',
     'pos', 'continue', the type names ('integer', 'string', etc.). Use
     tree-sitter precedence and contextual rules rather than reserving them
     globally. The formal reserved-word list (1.9) is the hard set; the rest
     are contextual.

  5. 'end X' KEYWORDS - SPACED vs FUSED.
     Block terminators accept both spaced and fused spellings:
       'end function' | 'endfunction', 'end sub' | 'endsub',
       'end if' | 'endif', 'end while' | 'endwhile',
       'end for' | 'endfor', 'end try' | 'endtry'.
     Also 'else if' | 'elseif', 'exit while' | 'exitwhile'.
     A bare 'end' (followed by a terminator) is the program-end statement and
     must be distinguished from the two-token block terminators by lookahead.

  6. BLOCK-IF vs SINGLE-LINE-IF DISAMBIGUATION.
     After 'if Expr [then]', if a NEWLINE follows you are in a block-if
     (terminated by 'end if'); if a statement follows on the same line it is a
     single-line-if (no 'end if'). The 'then' keyword is optional in both
     forms, which makes this purely a newline-driven decision - rely on the
     external scanner's terminator token. ELSE in a single-line-if is also
     inline; ELSE in a block-if introduces a new block.

  7. PRINT SEPARATORS & POSITIONAL FUNCTIONS.
     In a print statement, ';' and ',' are item separators (not operators);
     ',' advances to the next 16-char tab zone, ';' inserts nothing, and a
     TRAILING ';' or ',' suppresses the newline. TAB(n) and POS(n) are special
     positional items recognized only inside print. '?' is a full alias for
     'print'. Do not let the print-statement ';'/',' be parsed as expression
     operators; print has its own item-list rule.

  8. LABELS vs IDENTIFIER-COLON.
     A label is 'Identifier :' alone on a line. Distinguish from an AA key
     'Identifier :' which only occurs inside '{ }'. Bracket-depth context
     resolves this; at statement position a leading 'Identifier :' is a label.

  9. THE 'm' KEYWORD.
     'm' is the implicit per-instance associative array (like 'this'/'self').
     It is an ordinary identifier lexically but is conventionally highlighted
     and is always in scope inside functions/subs. No special parsing needed
     beyond optionally tagging it.

 10. COMMENTS.
     "'" and "rem" both start a line comment to end-of-line. 'rem' is a
     reserved word; ensure 'rem' at token start is a comment, while 'rem'
     appearing where an expression/AA-key is expected is just the keyword
     (it cannot be a variable name). Comments may appear at end of any line
     after a statement. Make comments an `extra` in tree-sitter.

 11. CONDITIONAL COMPILATION DIRECTIVES.
     '#const', '#if', '#else if', '#else', '#end if', '#error' begin with '#'
     at (effectively) line start and form their own line. The '#else if' and
     '#end if' are documented WITH a space; accept fused forms too. These are a
     compile-time preprocessing layer over boolean #const flags; for parsing
     purposes model them as statement-level constructs that wrap blocks (as in
     Part 4). They can appear at top level and inside function bodies.

 12. INTEGER vs FLOAT DIVISION & MOD.
     '/' is float division, '\' is integer division, 'mod' is the modulo
     keyword operator. Keep '\' distinct from any escape handling (BrightScript
     strings have no backslash escapes, so '\' is unambiguous outside strings).

 13. EXPONENTIATION ASSOCIATIVITY & UNARY MINUS.
     '^' is right-associative and binds TIGHTER than unary minus, so '-2^2'
     parses as '-(2^2)' = -4. Encode '^' with higher precedence than the
     unary +/- layer (see PowerExpr/UnaryExpr ordering in Part 5).

 14. OPTIONAL CHAINING & ++/-- & COMPOUND ASSIGN ARE VERSION-GATED.
     Optional chaining requires Roku OS 11.0+; '++'/'--' and compound
     assignment require Roku OS 7.1+; 'LongInteger' and '&' suffix require
     Roku OS 7.0+. They are included in this grammar unconditionally; gating is
     a target-version concern, not a parse concern.

 15. OPTIONAL-CALL '?()' IS EXPRESSION-ONLY (STATEMENT-VS-EXPRESSION).
     DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #9) on Roku OS 15.1.4: the optional-call
     suffix '?(args)' is valid only when it appears in EXPRESSION position
     (e.g. on the RHS of an assignment, 'y = f?()'). An optional-call used as a
     bare expression-STATEMENT (a line that is just 'o.fn?()') is a Syntax Error
     (&h02). The other optional-chaining suffixes ('?.', '?@', '?[') are not
     affected -- they are not statement termini in the first place. Practically:
     a plain CallSuffix '(...)' MAY end an ExpressionStatement, but OC_PAREN MAY
     NOT (see CallExpression in 3.14). The tree-sitter grammar should permit
     OC_PAREN only inside the PostfixExpr expression cascade (Part 5), not in the
     statement-level CallExpression rule. (Note this is independent of the
     '?'-vs-print and BrighterScript-ternary spacing rule in note 3.)

 16. NESTED NAMED FUNCTION DECLARATIONS ARE REJECTED.
     DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #5) on Roku OS 15.1.4: a named
     'function'/'sub' declaration is a TOP-LEVEL construct only. A named function
     declared inside another function body is a Syntax Error (&h02). Anonymous
     function VALUES (AnonymousFunction, 5.5) inside a body are fine. So
     FunctionDeclaration/SubDeclaration must be reachable from TopLevelItem but
     NOT from the in-body Statement set.

 17. 'as' TYPES ARE INTRINSIC-ONLY (a LINT rule, not a PARSE constraint).
     Structurally Type is ANY identifier: 'as <anything>' parses cleanly (see the
     Type rule's `Identifier` alternative in 2.5). The intrinsic-only restriction
     is enforced by a linter, not the grammar.
     DEVICE-CONFIRMED (DEVICE_FACTS.md #6, #7) on Roku OS 15.1.4: only the
     intrinsic type set (integer, longinteger, float, double, string, boolean,
     object, function, dynamic, void, invalid) is valid after 'as'. 'as interface'
     (&ha7) and 'as <ComponentType>' / any custom or component type name such as
     'as roSGNode' (&ha7) are rejected -- the latter are a BrighterScript
     transpile feature, not device BrightScript. A linter (not the parser) should
     flag a non-intrinsic 'as' type; the parser may still parse it (so it can be
     linted) but the spec marks it device-invalid (see the Type rule in 2.5).
*)

(*=== END OF GRAMMAR =========================================================*)
